      "message": "Even though we don\u0027t intend to ever display negative values, the formatting and casting here are technically wrong as well.\n\nThe upstream kernel supports a specific format specifier for `timespec64` now, but I think it\u0027s only available starting in 6.19.  And perhaps more importantly, I\u0027m not sure it correctly handles negative values either.\n\nI don\u0027t think we _need_ to print negative values correctly, since they can\u0027t logically exist here even if quirks of clock reading create them by accident.  So we don\u0027t need to worry about printing { -5, 100000000 } as -4.9.  But we still shouldn\u0027t be casting/printing signed values as unsigned, which - as we\u0027ve encountered - masks unexpected values of one type that would be easy to diagnose as unexpected values of an entirely different type that take a bit more work.\n\nSo I propose that the full fix:\n\n* sets a floor of 0.0 for `sleep_time`, as you have done\n* also sets a floor of 0.0 for `suspend_resume_time`, to proactively avoid future issues there\n* stops casting and printing the values as unsigned (which they are not)",
